We’re following the Harrow-facsimile. It looks like him, but there’s a coldness in his eyes, a detached demeanor, and a strange, knowing tone to his words. He keeps referring to the group as "the //six// of us." It's unsettling. //[Voice lowering.]// He also hasn't blinked. Not once. 



He's led us to the banks of a wide, emerald-green river some ways north or northeast of the wreck and away from the pods. Deep, moderate current. Banks and surrounding land is a silty-black with small specks of something metallic peppered in. This isn't—//[Louder, to the others.]// I don't think this is passable. 



He's— The facsimile, rather, is collecting these large, dried leaves from the nearby treeline. //[Pauses.]// It's now approaching organic growths along the riverbank and is retrieving a clear paste from inside them. //[Pauses.]// Coating the underside of the leaves in this paste, and—  //[Pauses.]// Covering said paste with the metallic silt that comprises the ground around us. 



//[Silence.]//



I see. The facsimile pushed one of the leaves into the river, perpendicular its flow, sending the makeshift boat straight to the other side, as if there were no current at all. Another chemical reaction, perhaps? Magnetic forces attracted to minerals in the riverbed? 



Either way, we're doing the same, now. 

[[/div]]

